{
  "id": "80907",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Isomerism in Complexes",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Isomers are different chemical species that have the same chemical formula. Transition metal complexes often exist as "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "geometric isomers"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Isomers that differ in the way in which atoms are oriented in space relative to each other, leading to different physical and chemical properties."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1448747103",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1604895932",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", in which the same atoms are connected through the same types of bonds but with differences in their orientation in space. Coordination complexes with two different ligands in the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "cis"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "trans"
              },
              {
                "text": " positions from a ligand of interest form isomers. For example, the octahedral complex [Co(NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "Cl"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "] ion has two isomers. In the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "cis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " configuration"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Configuration of a geometrical isomer in which two similar groups are on the same side of an imaginary reference line on the molecule."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1966077778",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "694835887",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", the two chloride ligands are adjacent to each other. The other isomer, the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "trans"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " configuration"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Configuration of a geometrical isomer in which two similar groups are on opposite sides of an imaginary reference line on the molecule."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2801983027",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2532949214",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", has the two chloride ligands directly across from one another."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3087088366",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "The "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "cis"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " and "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "trans"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " isomers of [Co(H"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "O)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Cl"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " contain the same ligands attached to the same metal ion, but the spatial arrangement causes these two compounds to have different properties."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "436407765",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "vu8varyy2sifr94",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "id": "1970587296",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/95/95EBBA8434D3DAFB7CC80081D3E26FB2/cis_trans_forms_updated.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 400
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "1991568007",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "3809233136",
        "textDirection": "ltr",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Different geometric isomers of a substance are different chemical compounds. They exhibit different properties even though they have the same formula. For example, the two isomers of [Co(NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "Cl"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "]NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": " differ in color: the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "cis"
              },
              {
                "text": " form is violet, and the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "trans"
              },
              {
                "text": " form is green. Furthermore, these isomers have different dipole moments, solubilities, and reactivities. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "2493330489",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "As an example of how the arrangement in space can influence the molecular properties, consider the polarity of the two [Co(NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "Cl"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "]NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": " isomers. Remember that the polarity of a molecule or ion is determined by the bond dipoles (which are due to the difference in electronegativity of the bonding atoms) and their arrangement in space. In one isomer, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "cis"
              },
              {
                "text": " chloride ligands cause more electron density on one side of the molecule than on the other, making it polar. For the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "trans"
              },
              {
                "text": " isomer, each ligand is directly across from an identical ligand, so the bond dipoles cancel out, and the molecule is nonpolar."
              }
            ],
            "id": "iuf7o6w4bk4c82d",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "3005957129",
        "textDirection": "ltr",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "In 1965, scientists at Michigan State University discovered that one of the isomers of the compound [Pt(NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ")"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Cl"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "] inhibited cell division in certain microorganisms, indicating that this isomer could be an anticancer agent. In 1978, the US Food and Drug Administration approved this compound for the treatment of certain forms of cancer."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "315801098",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "This isomer is shown below. Identify which geometric isomer it is, and give its full name. Draw the other geometric isomer and name it."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "916962656",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "umxe2ns5m96kz8a",
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "display": "block",
                "height": 131.0625,
                "id": "1288953664",
                "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/E5/E565473D8C64BFEE89DF002D03578581/cisplatin.png",
                "type": "img",
                "width": 175
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3739638040",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "editor": "slate",
            "id": "2474360067",
            "textDirection": "ltr",
            "type": "content"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "Solution"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "77804356",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "In the figure above, the two chlorine ligands are next to each other—they occupy "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "cis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " positions—so this is the "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "cis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " isomer."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "14913283",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "This isomer has two NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and two Cl"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "–"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ligands attached to the central Pt"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ion. When naming specific isomers, the descriptor is listed in front of the name. This isomer is therefore named "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "cis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "-diamminedichloroplatinum(II)."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3176528980",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "trans"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " isomer of [Pt(NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ")"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Cl"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "] has each ligand directly across from an adjacent ligand, as shown below."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3237725244",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "o6h2n5nspxvu9ew",
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "display": "block",
                "height": 82.0078125,
                "id": "3028519890",
                "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/B9/B92D324BD00384969CE79A5CAC7901B9/Screenshot_2023-10-08_at_52943_pm.png",
                "type": "img",
                "width": 250
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "529138726",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "editor": "slate",
            "id": "1679232736",
            "textDirection": "ltr",
            "type": "content"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The name of this isomer is "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "trans"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "-diamminedichloroplatinum(II). (Note: This isomer has no cancer-fighting properties)."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2070760499",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Since this discovery, many similar platinum compounds have been developed for the treatment of cancer. In all cases, they are the "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "cis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " isomers and never the "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "trans"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " isomers. The diammine (NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ")"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " portion is retained with other groups, replacing the dichloro [(Cl)"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "] portion. The newer drugs include carboplatin, oxaliplatin, and satraplatin."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3189884510",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "editor": "slate",
            "id": "3624862748",
            "textDirection": "ltr",
            "type": "content"
          }
        ],
        "id": "2339350915",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "example",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Draw the complex ion "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "trans"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "-diaqua-"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "trans"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "-dibromo-"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "trans"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "-dichlorocobalt(II). Then answer the following questions."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4076220205",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "editor": "slate",
