        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Another important type of isomer is the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "optical isomer"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A molecule that is a nonsuperimposable mirror image with identical chemical and physical properties, except when it reacts with other optical isomers. Also called "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "enantiomer"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1713829306",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "109494337",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", or "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "enantiomer"
              },
              {
                "text": ", in which two objects are exact mirror images of each other but cannot be lined up so that all parts match. This means that optical isomers are nonsuperimposable mirror images. A classic example of this is a pair of hands, in which the right and left hand are mirror images of one another but cannot be superimposed."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1953298824",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "5okl6malz6rqryw",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "id": "786872365",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/89/896ADD65B8ADCA1A0DAA3FCB267C7F37/optical_isomers.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Optical isomers are very important in organic and biochemistry because living systems often incorporate one specific optical isomer and not the other. Unlike geometric isomers, pairs of optical isomers have identical properties (such as boiling point, polarity, solubility). Optical isomers differ only in the way they affect polarized light and how they react with other optical isomers. For coordination complexes, many coordination compounds such as [M(en)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "n+"
              },
              {
                "text": ", in which M"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "n+"
              },
              {
                "text": " is a central metal ion such as iron(III) or cobalt(II), form enantiomers, as shown below. These two isomers will react differently with other optical isomers. For example, DNA helices are optical isomers, and the form that occurs in nature (right-handed DNA) will bind to only one isomer of [M(en)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "n+"
              },
              {
                "text": " and not the other."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3119240966",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "The complex [M(en)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "n+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " (M"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "n+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " = a metal ion, en = ethylenediamine) has a nonsuperimposable mirror image and is chiral."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2216424195",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "kdrvmpuemtlnntj",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "height": 163.0078125,
            "id": "2531046063",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/1F/1FFF9F6AE9E318E8F21736AD5A5D44C7/M(en)3.jpeg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 600
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "1491655441",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "2057965665",
        "textDirection": "ltr",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The [Co(en)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "Cl"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "]+ ion exhibits geometric isomerism ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "cis"
              },
              {
                "text": "/"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "trans"
              },
              {
                "text": "), and its "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "cis"
              },
              {
                "text": " isomer exists as a pair of optical isomers (see image below)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3223904683",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Three isomeric forms of [Co(en)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Cl"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " exist. The "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "trans "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "isomer, formed when the chlorines are positioned at a 180° angle, has very different properties from the "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "cis "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "isomers. The mirror images of the "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "cis "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "isomer form a pair of optical isomers, which have identical behavior except when reacting with other enantiomers."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2593270547",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "0iygdfwnur85u5g",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
