      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "linkage isomer"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Coordination compound with the same composition but with a ligand that can bind to the transition metal in different ways (such as CN"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "−"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " vs. NC"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "−"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ")."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2732648826",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "202927892",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " occurs when the coordination compound contains a ligand that can bind to the transition metal center through two different atoms. For example, the CN"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " ligand can bind through the carbon atom (cyano) or through the nitrogen atom (isocyano). Similarly, SCN"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " can be bound through the sulfur or nitrogen atom, affording two distinct compounds ([Co(NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "5"
              },
              {
                "text": "SCN]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": " or [Co(NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "5"
              },
              {
                "text": "NCS]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": ")."
              }
            ],
            "id": "831973431",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "An "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "ionization isomer"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An isomer in which a ligand has exchanged places with an anion or molecule that was originally outside the coordination sphere."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "564669305",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3854952178",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " occurs when one anionic ligand in the inner coordination sphere is replaced with the counterion from the outer coordination sphere. [CoCl"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": "]Br and [CoCl"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "5"
              },
              {
                "text": "Br]Cl are examples of ionization isomers. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "2738270428",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Coordination isomers occur when a different metal has the same coordination environment of ligands as another, often from two metals in a complex compound changing places. [Cu(NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "][CoCl"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "] and [Co(NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "][CuCl"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "] are examples of coordination isomers."
              }
            ],
            "id": "137501461",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
